TWO AUDIENCES, TWO MECHANISMS -- the ruling's own separation, and collapsing
them is the error it corrects. $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hypergumbo/ belongs to the
NORMAL user and is reached by an explicit subcommand (WI-talaz). ~/hypergumbo is
something else: a literal `hypergumbo` directory in a home directory is a
repository CHECKOUT, and nobody makes one by accident, which is what turns its
presence into a deliberate signal a config directory can never be.

When one is present, hypergumbo offers ONCE to place EXAMPLE repo-tier overlay
files in ~/hypergumbo/.hypergumbo-examples/ -- the material a developer needs to
see what a <repo>/.hypergumbo/ would contain, WITHOUT one being written into any
repository.

THE THREE CONSTRAINTS, each a defect if dropped, and two of them asserted as
things that must NOT happen:
  * Nothing is ever written into an analysed repository. Containment is
    asserted in the writer, not promised in a docstring -- that function is the
    one whose bug puts a file in somebody's tree.
  * A decline is RECORDED, so the offer goes quiet. The three-way
    accepted/declined/never-asked return is the point: folding a decline into a
    falsy "not accepted" re-asks forever, which is the nag ADR-0045 ruling 8
    forbids and which this project has already written down as corrosive.
  * Never raised when stdin is not a TTY. The gate is on STDIN, not stderr:
    cli.py's existing isatty checks gate RENDERING, and an offer needs an
    ANSWER, so the stream that must be a terminal is the one the answer arrives
    on. A prompt that blocks a CI run or an agent invocation is a defect.

WHERE THE DECISION LIVES, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE TRUST STORE. This item's
instruction was to reuse ADR-0045 ruling 8's store. THAT IS NOT SATISFIABLE:
backend_trust.record_decision raises for any key outside
BACKENDS_EXECUTING_ANALYSED_CODE -- frozenset({'rust_analyzer'}) -- on the
grounds that a non-executing opt-in is a preference and belongs in the
configuration file, and the configuration file is the one ADR-0045 says the tool
may READ and must NOT write. All three doors were shut, so the conflict went to
the owner rather than being guessed at.

OWNER'S RULING (2026-08-28): a separate UX-state record, kept clearly apart from
the trust store. A UX decline is categorically not a trust grant, and that
refusal is a SECURITY guard that should not be widened to carry a prompt
preference. So the answer lives at $XDG_STATE_HOME/hypergumbo/offers.json, a
SIBLING of trust.d. The separation is pinned structurally -- same state root,
never nested inside the store, never inside the portable config directory -- and
a test asserts the trust store STILL REFUSES this key, so a later change to that
ruling surfaces here instead of leaving a stale second home behind.

A FAILING OFFER CAN NEVER BREAK A RUN. The call site swallows everything: being
un-asked is the pre-feature behaviour and is never wrong, while a courtesy that
turns a successful analysis into a traceback is strictly worse than no courtesy.
It is called at the dispatch return, AFTER the command has produced its output,
so an offer never interleaves with the thing the user actually ran -- and not
inside the analysis pipeline, which is reached from many entry points and is the
wrong place to read stdin.

Defaults to NO: only an explicit affirmative counts, so a stray keystroke writes
nothing. The prompt names the exact destination before anyone answers, because
~/hypergumbo is a git working tree and someone typing 'y' should know where the
files land before they type it -- the examples directory is dot-prefixed and
tool-named for the same reason, and its README says it is safe to delete and
suggests .git/info/exclude.

Coverage: repo_tier_offer.py 100%, and every new line of the CLI wrapper.
